The work and its supervision
----------------------------

Research internship at the Laboratoire Jean Kuntzmann (LJK), Université
Grenoble Alpes, 21 May to 15 July 2024, supervised by **Jean-Guillaume Dumas**.
The two PDFs under original/ are my own write-ups of algorithms taken from the
papers cited below; the derivations and the worked examples are mine, the
algorithms are theirs.

The original working repository is on the UGA GitLab (hamlilm/AltBase) and is
not publicly reachable. This is the public copy.


Papers the algorithms come from
-------------------------------

**references.md** names every one of them, with the numbered result each piece
of code implements. It is the only place citations live, so that a claim can be
checked against its source rather than against the code implementing it.

The framing, the notation and the algorithm numbering the sparsification strand
inherited come from original/Sparsifying_Matrices/matrix_sparsification_summary.pdf,
which is my own write-up of algorithms that are not mine.

One credit belongs here rather than only in a bibliography, because it is
unpublished and was given directly: the determinant-polynomial feasibility test
in matrix_sparsification/pattern_feasibility.h, and the operator in
fixtures/dumas_counterexample_l.matrix, are from a Maple worksheet by
Jean-Guillaume Dumas (cex_poldet, 27 May 2024) supplied with the internship
material. The construction is his; the C++ and the tests are mine.


Software this depends on
------------------------

  * **Givaro**: exact arithmetic over finite fields and over the rationals,
    developed by the CASYS team at the Laboratoire Jean Kuntzmann, Université
    Grenoble Alpes. https://casys.gricad-pages.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/givaro/
    Used, not vendored: it is installed from the system package manager and no
    part of it is copied into this repository.

  * **GMP**, on which Givaro's integer and rational arithmetic rests.

FFLAS-FFPACK, from the same team, was used for rank in an earlier revision and
removed once a reduction against an echelon-form basis measured faster at these
sizes. It is mentioned in the history and is no longer a dependency.


Timings
-------

Every time reported in this repository was measured on a single core of a 12th
Gen Intel Core i5-12450H at 2.2 GHz, which is the machine Table 1 of
original/Bilinear_Rank_over_Finite_Field/bilinear_rank_problem_heuristic.pdf was
measured on. Comparisons between the two are therefore like for like on
hardware, but not on language: the internship's column is Julia and this
repository's is C++.
